    {
      "id": "Z-5vDs6tNbrIhza0wNfH9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 474.7849926275473,
      "y": 282.5160962128757,
      "width": 1187.0382080078125,
      "height": 1025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 40300050,
      "version": 2240,
      "versionNonce": 734658666,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782188068859,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Design a Rate Limiter\n\nA rate limiter controls how many requests a client can make within a specific timeframe.\nIt acts like a traffic controller for your API - allowingm, for example, 100 requests per minute from a user,\nthen rejecting excess requests with an HTTP 429 \"Too Many Requests\" response. Rate limiters prevent abuse,\nprotect your servers from being overwhelmed by bursts of traffic, and ensure fair usage accross all users.\n\n\nFunctional Requirements \n- identify users by id, IP or API key\n- limit requests based on configurable rules\n- return proper error headers and status codes\n\n\nScale  (Always check in with the interviewer with the scale, especially in the case of rate limiter)\n(What are we rate limiting? A social media app- Facebook? A developer tool? An intra micro services)\n- 100M DAU\n- 1M rps\n\n(We're building a Server Side Rate Limiter)\n\nNon Functional Requirements (xility - scalibility, durability....)\n- availability >> consistency (Always start with CAP theorem - Partition tolerance is must)\n(If a new config is pushed - do we want the Rate Limiter to be down and take the new config\nor still run with the old configuration)\n- low latency rate limit checks (< 10ms)\n- scalable to 1M rps (We are qualifying and quantifying both :) )\n\n(Confirm with the interviewer that are we missing something we need to focus on)\n(We need to satisfy the above 6 with our design)\n\n\nStep 2 - Core Entities\n- Request (Incoming Request)\n- Client (IP, userId, Api key)\n- Rules (100 r/s)\n\nStep 3 - System Interface (Instead of APIs as its not a customer facing appln - user's are not calling this API, but it's us who is doing\nthe rate limits)\n\n- isRequestAllowed(clientId, rulesId) -> { passes: boolean, remaining: number, resetTime: timestamp }",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Design a Rate Limiter\n\nA rate limiter controls how many requests a client can make within a specific timeframe.\nIt acts like a traffic controller for your API - allowingm, for example, 100 requests per minute from a user,\nthen rejecting excess requests with an HTTP 429 \"Too Many Requests\" response. Rate limiters prevent abuse,\nprotect your servers from being overwhelmed by bursts of traffic, and ensure fair usage accross all users.\n\n\nFunctional Requirements \n- identify users by id, IP or API key\n- limit requests based on configurable rules\n- return proper error headers and status codes\n\n\nScale  (Always check in with the interviewer with the scale, especially in the case of rate limiter)\n(What are we rate limiting? A social media app- Facebook? A developer tool? An intra micro services)\n- 100M DAU\n- 1M rps\n\n(We're building a Server Side Rate Limiter)\n\nNon Functional Requirements (xility - scalibility, durability....)\n- availability >> consistency (Always start with CAP theorem - Partition tolerance is must)\n(If a new config is pushed - do we want the Rate Limiter to be down and take the new config\nor still run with the old configuration)\n- low latency rate limit checks (< 10ms)\n- scalable to 1M rps (We are qualifying and quantifying both :) )\n\n(Confirm with the interviewer that are we missing something we need to focus on)\n(We need to satisfy the above 6 with our design)\n\n\nStep 2 - Core Entities\n- Request (Incoming Request)\n- Client (IP, userId, Api key)\n- Rules (100 r/s)\n\nStep 3 - System Interface (Instead of APIs as its not a customer facing appln - user's are not calling this API, but it's us who is doing\nthe rate limits)\n\n- isRequestAllowed(clientId, rulesId) -> { passes: boolean, remaining: number, resetTime: timestamp }",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
